<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Königsegg</h1><p class="st-meta">1629 CE – 1806 CE · capital Aulendorf</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Upper Swabian county of the Königsegg family, imperial counts who served the Habsburgs as soldiers and diplomats.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Königsegg family, Swabian ministerials named after a castle near Saulgau, accumulated lordships around Aulendorf and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allgau">Allgäu</a>. In 1629 they were raised to imperial counts, and the lands were divided between the lines of Königsegg-Aulendorf and Königsegg-Rothenfels, the latter centred on Immenstadt.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counties were small Catholic territories held within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-circle">Swabian Circle</a>, administered from Aulendorf Castle. Members of the family pursued careers in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-habsburg">Habsburg</a> service: Lothar Joseph Dominik von Königsegg-Rothenfels was an imperial field marshal and diplomat, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-friedrich-von-konigsegg-rothenfels">Maximilian Friedrich von Königsegg-Rothenfels</a> became archbishop-elector of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-cologne">Cologne</a> in 1761.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Königsegg-Rothenfels was exchanged with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> for estates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in 1804. Königsegg-Aulendorf was mediatised in 1806 and placed under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg</a>, the family keeping its estates and castle.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="county, 1629 CE – 1806 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">county</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.12%;width:3.52%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1629 CE – 1806 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aulendorf</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Königsegg-Aulendorf, Königsegg-Rothenfels</dd></dl></aside></div>
